Box 2| Defining the multifunctional efficiency of nanocarriers
Nanocarriers for drug delivery have to fulfillnumerous tasks on their way through the human body (Box 1). The nanomaterials have to be multifunctional in order to comply with diverse requirements.However, the production process of these highly sophisticated and complex materials should be as facile and robust as possible to ensure reproducible quality,cost effectiveness, and technical feasibility in large scales.Here, we define the parameter‘multifunctional eficiency for the assessment of nanocarriers with consideration of their ‘functionality ratio and ‘process efficiency':
(1）A functional unit (FU) is defined as a distinct building unit (BU) of the nanomaterial, providing properties that qualify for the intended specific purpose as a nanocarrier or that fulfill specific tasks within the drug-delivery pathway. Examples are shielding domains, targeting ligands, endosomolytic elements, controlled release coatings or chemical valves, and the therapeutic entities.
(2)A process step (PRS) is defined as a distinct step within the preparation process that converts the product of the preceding PRS and readily available reactants into an isolatable next product.Examples include the formation of nanoparticles in a one-step self-assembly reaction, post-modification by polymer conjugation or surface coating, and drug loading.
(3） The functionality ratio (FR) of a nanocarrier is defined according to formula $(i)$
$$
F R:=\frac{n_{F U}}{m_{B U}}
$$
where $n_{F U}$ is the number of functional units and $m_{B U}$ is the total number of building units of the nanocarrier.
(4）The process eficiency (PE)of a nanocarrier-production process is defined according to formula (ii)
$$
P E:=\frac{n_{F U}}{r_{P R S}}
$$
where $n_{F U}$ is the number of FUs and $r_{P R S}$ is the number of PRSs within the production process of the nanocarrier.
(5） The multifunctional efficiency (MFE) is defined according to formula (iii)
$$
(i i i)\quad M F E:=F R\cdot P E=\frac{{n_{F U}}^{2}}{m_{B U}\cdot r_{P R S}}
$$
where $F R$ is the functionality ratio, $P E$ is the process efficiency, $n_{F U}$ is the number of functional units, $m_{B U}$ is the total number of building units and $r_{P R S}$ is the number of process steps within the production process of the nanocarrier.
